1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.629
(narrator) Audio-described version: before each section, the picture pauses while I describe it.

2
00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:08.701
(narrator) The intro: a huge Y2K, then a red stamp, the bug.

3
00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:16.851
(narrator) A memory chip with a price tag shows that memory was expensive, so the year 1985 loses its first two digits and only 85 is stored.

4
00:00:16.851 --> 00:00:23.184
(narrator) A two-digit field rolls from 99 to 00, and a code card shows the computer reading 19 plus 00 as 1900.

5
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:28.074
(narrator) Another code card computes an age as 00 minus 85, which is minus 85 years old.

6
00:00:28.074 --> 00:00:36.663
(narrator) Twelve programs, from payroll to grades, all carry the same two-digit year tag and turn red; a search for YY finds it in file after file.

7
00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:43.183
(narrator) A clock turns over to January 1, 2000, almost nothing breaks, and an invoice totals billions of dollars.

8
00:00:43.183 --> 00:00:49.327
(narrator) A case-file folder gives the title, Every module keeps a secret, while your hero sits at a laptop.

9
00:00:49.327 --> 00:00:54.093
[music]

10
00:00:54.093 --> 00:00:57.473
Y2K. No, not the aesthetic. The bug.

11
00:00:57.853 --> 00:01:01.233
Back in the sixties, memory was expensive.

12
00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:04.173
So programmers saved space:

13
00:01:04.173 --> 00:01:07.313
they stored the year with just two digits.

14
00:01:08.053 --> 00:01:10.813
1985? Just “85.”

15
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:15.113
Then comes the year 2000: “double-zero.”

16
00:01:15.213 --> 00:01:18.233
And the computer reads it as… 1900.

17
00:01:18.373 --> 00:01:22.313
Born in ’85? Zero minus 85:

18
00:01:22.493 --> 00:01:24.853
you’re negative 85 years old.

19
00:01:24.853 --> 00:01:29.213
Nobody thought this code would still run in 2000.

20
00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:30.873
It did.

21
00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:34.473
And the two-digit year wasn’t hidden in one module.

22
00:01:34.473 --> 00:01:37.653
It was copied into every calculation.

23
00:01:37.853 --> 00:01:40.393
So engineers spent years hunting down

24
00:01:40.393 --> 00:01:42.553
every line that touched a year.

25
00:01:42.553 --> 00:01:45.773
Midnight came… and almost nothing broke,

26
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:48.013
because they fixed it in time.

27
00:01:48.013 --> 00:01:51.693
The bill? Billions.

28
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:56.073
Lesson one: every module keeps a secret.

29
00:01:56.073 --> 00:02:09.433
[music]

30
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:10.764
(narrator) Verse 1, why it matters.

31
00:02:10.764 --> 00:02:16.751
(narrator) A printed stack of Apollo code grows as tall as a person; a car’s counter runs to 100 million lines of code.

32
00:02:16.751 --> 00:02:19.633
(narrator) A head overflows with pages: no one can hold it all.

33
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:23.791
(narrator) Four modules with every connection drawn make 6 connections; eight make 28.

34
00:02:23.791 --> 00:02:28.059
(narrator) When everything depends on everything, the web clumps into a Big Ball of Mud.

35
00:02:28.059 --> 00:02:32.716
(narrator) One changed file turns eleven more red, and squashing one bug makes two more appear.

36
00:02:33.313 --> 00:02:35.833
♪ Apollo’s code, printed out, ♪

37
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:38.033
♪ stood as tall as Margaret Hamilton. ♪

38
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:41.433
♪ A modern car? A hundred million lines. ♪

39
00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:44.093
♪ We’re not a thousand times smarter than back then, ♪

40
00:02:44.093 --> 00:02:47.073
♪ so no one holds the whole thing in their head. ♪

41
00:02:47.073 --> 00:02:49.333
♪ Four modules? Six connections. ♪

42
00:02:49.333 --> 00:02:51.953
♪ Eight modules? Twenty-eight. ♪

43
00:02:51.953 --> 00:02:54.973
♪ Let every part depend on every other part, ♪

44
00:02:54.973 --> 00:02:57.793
♪ and you’ve built a Big Ball of Mud: ♪

45
00:02:57.793 --> 00:03:00.793
♪ change one thing, touch dozens of files; ♪

46
00:03:00.793 --> 00:03:05.513
♪ fix one bug, and two more appear. ♪

47
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.615
(narrator) Pre-chorus: how to split a system.

48
00:03:08.615 --> 00:03:13.500
(narrator) A flowchart of steps, read, process, print, gets a red stamp: not by steps.

49
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:20.470
(narrator) Then the title page of Parnas’s 1972 paper, On the Criteria To Be Used in Decomposing Systems into Modules.

50
00:03:21.093 --> 00:03:24.853
♪ So how do you split a system into modules? ♪

51
00:03:24.853 --> 00:03:27.653
♪ Not by the steps it runs: first this, then that. ♪

52
00:03:27.653 --> 00:03:32.553
♪ In 1972, David Parnas wrote: ♪

53
00:03:32.553 --> 00:03:34.067
[music]

54
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:41.680
(narrator) The chorus: a card shows Parnas’s sentence line by line, with difficult design decisions in gold and likely to change in cyan.

55
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:48.266
(narrator) Three modules, Dates, PaymentGateway, and UserRepository, each get a red secret tab, with walls between them.

56
00:03:48.266 --> 00:03:56.181
(narrator) Then one module, Dates, is split in two layers: the interface, year of date, on top, and the secret, how the year is stored, below.

57
00:03:56.181 --> 00:04:04.338
(narrator) The secret changes from 85 to 1985, the interface stays the same, the callers stay unchanged, and a stamp says only one module changed.

58
00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:07.393
♪ We propose that one begins ♪

59
00:04:07.393 --> 00:04:10.393
♪ with a list of difficult design decisions ♪

60
00:04:10.393 --> 00:04:13.233
♪ or design decisions which are likely to change. ♪

61
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:15.433
♪ Each module is then designed ♪

62
00:04:15.433 --> 00:04:18.393
♪ to hide such a decision from the others. ♪

63
00:04:18.393 --> 00:04:20.393
♪ (From the others!) Every module keeps a secret. ♪

64
00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:21.833
♪ Show the what, and hide the how. ♪

65
00:04:21.833 --> 00:04:23.253
♪ Change the how, but keep the what — ♪

66
00:04:23.253 --> 00:04:27.500
♪ (but keep the what!) You change one… one module. (Only one!) ♪

67
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.267
(narrator) Verse 2, the PayPal tangle.

68
00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:38.720
(narrator) Your hero builds a store with three services, OrderService, RefundService, and WalletService; each calls the PayPal SDK directly and carries the same red PayPal decision tab.

69
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.338
(narrator) The boss says we’re switching to Stripe, and a search for PayPal finds it everywhere.

70
00:04:43.338 --> 00:04:49.911
(narrator) Then the tangle disappears: a PaymentGateway interface with charge and refund sits between the services and the provider.

71
00:04:49.911 --> 00:04:53.443
(narrator) Only a PayPalGateway module, below it, carries the PayPal secret.

72
00:04:53.443 --> 00:04:57.463
(narrator) A StripeGateway plugs in beside it, and all three services stay unchanged.

73
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.980
♪ Your team builds a store: checkout, refunds, a wallet. ♪

74
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.860
♪ All three services call PayPal directly. ♪

75
00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.640
♪ Three services, and all three know the secret. ♪

76
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.440
♪ Then the boss walks in: “We’re switching to Stripe!” ♪

77
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.040
♪ You search the code for PayPal… (It’s everywhere!) ♪

78
00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.620
♪ So hide it: one interface, Payment Gateway. ♪

79
00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.500
♪ Charge and refund. No PayPal in sight. ♪

80
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.720
♪ (The name’s Gateway. Payment Gateway.) ♪

81
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.060
♪ Only PayPal Gateway knows that PayPal exists. ♪

82
00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:26.900
♪ Stripe? Write a Stripe Gateway and plug it in: ♪

83
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.640
♪ the three services don’t change at all. ♪

84
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.333
[music]

85
00:05:32.333 --> 00:05:35.625
(narrator) Pre-chorus 2, the payoff Parnas promised: three panels.

86
00:05:35.625 --> 00:05:38.977
(narrator) Flexibility, change one module and leave the rest alone.

87
00:05:38.977 --> 00:05:41.730
(narrator) Comprehensibility, study one module at a time.

88
00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:45.261
(narrator) Parallel work, teams side by side with little need to talk.

89
00:05:45.853 --> 00:05:48.373
♪ That’s the payoff Parnas promised: ♪

90
00:05:48.373 --> 00:05:51.233
♪ change one module, leave the rest alone; ♪

91
00:05:51.233 --> 00:05:54.073
♪ study the system one module at a time; ♪

92
00:05:54.073 --> 00:05:57.433
♪ split the work, and teams build side by side ♪

93
00:05:57.433 --> 00:05:59.693
♪ with little need to talk. ♪

94
00:05:59.693 --> 00:06:01.467
[music]

95
00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:05.804
(narrator) The chorus again, with Parnas’s sentence and the three modules with their secrets.

96
00:06:05.804 --> 00:06:15.219
(narrator) This time the split module is PaymentGateway: its interface, charge order and payment, stays the same while its secret changes from PayPal to Stripe, and only one module changes.

97
00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.940
♪ We propose that one begins ♪

98
00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.980
♪ with a list of difficult design decisions ♪

99
00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.820
♪ or design decisions which are likely to change. ♪

100
00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.960
♪ Each module has been designed ♪

101
00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.940
♪ to hide such a decision from the other. ♪

102
00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.040
♪ Every module keeps a secret. ♪

103
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.480
♪ Show the what, and hide the how. ♪

104
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.480
♪ Change the how, but keep the what: ♪

105
00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:38.167
♪ you change one module. (Only one!) ♪

106
00:06:38.467 --> 00:06:39.621
(narrator) Verse 3, the iceberg.

107
00:06:39.621 --> 00:06:49.406
(narrator) The tip above the water is labeled interface, a stable contract; the bulk below is the implementation, where turning gears are swapped for new ones while the tip stays unchanged.

108
00:06:49.406 --> 00:06:55.342
(narrator) Below the waterline, three choice toggles flip: array or hash table, cache or no cache, MongoDB or Supabase.

109
00:06:55.342 --> 00:07:02.763
(narrator) Then a cutaway wall: a phone charges from an outlet while, behind the wall, solar panels at noon give way to wind turbines at midnight.

110
00:07:02.763 --> 00:07:06.006
(narrator) The outlet is the interface; the power plant is the secret.

111
00:07:06.607 --> 00:07:09.207
♪ Picture an iceberg. The tip’s the interface: ♪

112
00:07:09.207 --> 00:07:12.067
♪ a stable contract, what the module does. ♪

113
00:07:12.067 --> 00:07:14.547
♪ Under the water, the implementation: ♪

114
00:07:14.547 --> 00:07:17.247
♪ how the module keeps its promise. ♪

115
00:07:17.247 --> 00:07:20.907
♪ Change it freely, as long as the tip stays the same. ♪

116
00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:23.627
♪ What goes below? The choices that could change: ♪

117
00:07:23.627 --> 00:07:26.547
♪ array or hash table? Cache or no cache? ♪

118
00:07:26.547 --> 00:07:29.627
♪ Mongo or Supabase? Pick one, and hide it, ♪

119
00:07:29.627 --> 00:07:31.707
♪ so later you can change your mind. ♪

120
00:07:31.707 --> 00:07:34.267
♪ Your charger can’t tell where the power’s from: ♪

121
00:07:34.267 --> 00:07:37.447
♪ sun at noon, or wind at midnight. ♪

122
00:07:37.447 --> 00:07:42.327
♪ The outlet’s the interface. The plant’s the secret. ♪

123
00:07:43.533 --> 00:07:45.279
(narrator) The bridge, private isn’t enough.

124
00:07:45.279 --> 00:07:48.242
(narrator) Your hero thinks every field is private, so it’s hidden.

125
00:07:48.242 --> 00:07:56.919
(narrator) A code card shows the private fields, but the public checkout method returns a PayPalCharge and takes a PayPalAccount, all outlined in red: leaked in the signature.

126
00:07:56.919 --> 00:08:00.411
(narrator) Hiding data gets a stamp, not it; hiding decisions lights up gold.

127
00:08:00.411 --> 00:08:09.089
(narrator) Then a client and a server are joined by a visible contract, the server’s database choice stays hidden inside it, and stateless or stateful drops onto the contract.

128
00:08:09.693 --> 00:08:13.533
♪ “I made every field private. So it’s hidden, right?” ♪

129
00:08:13.673 --> 00:08:16.893
♪ But checkout still returns a PayPal Charge: ♪

130
00:08:16.893 --> 00:08:19.953
♪ the secret’s in the signature, in plain sight. ♪

131
00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:22.233
♪ It’s not about hiding data. ♪

132
00:08:22.233 --> 00:08:25.473
♪ It’s about hiding decisions. (Decisions!) ♪

133
00:08:25.473 --> 00:08:28.093
♪ And don’t hide what callers need to know: ♪

134
00:08:28.093 --> 00:08:32.673
♪ stateless or stateful? That goes in the contract. ♪

135
00:08:32.673 --> 00:08:34.633
[music]

136
00:08:34.633 --> 00:08:39.905
(narrator) The change test: a checklist, list the likely changes, pick one, count the modules it touches.

137
00:08:39.905 --> 00:08:47.477
(narrator) For a new payment provider, a well-hidden design lights only the gateway, you’re good; a leaky design lights all six modules, redesign.

138
00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.040
♪ Not sure you hid it well? Run the change test: ♪

139
00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.260
♪ list the changes likely to come. ♪

140
00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:57.640
♪ Pick one. Count the modules that would change. ♪

141
00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.667
♪ Just one? (You’re good!) Dozens? (Redesign!) ♪

142
00:09:01.967 --> 00:09:05.520
(narrator) The pop quiz: seven cards, each with a question and then the answer.

143
00:09:06.107 --> 00:09:10.727
♪ Pop quiz, agent! Y2K: what got copied everywhere? ♪

144
00:09:10.727 --> 00:09:12.027
♪ (crowd) The two-digit year! ♪

145
00:09:12.027 --> 00:09:13.367
♪ What should a module hide? ♪

146
00:09:13.367 --> 00:09:15.067
♪ (crowd) A decision likely to change! ♪

147
00:09:15.067 --> 00:09:17.947
♪ The interface shows the what… or the how? ♪

148
00:09:17.947 --> 00:09:18.547
♪ (crowd) The what! ♪

149
00:09:18.547 --> 00:09:20.927
♪ Every field is private. Is the decision hidden? ♪

150
00:09:20.927 --> 00:09:22.467
♪ (crowd) Not if the signature leaks it! ♪

151
00:09:22.467 --> 00:09:25.167
♪ Your server’s database: show it, or hide it? ♪

152
00:09:25.167 --> 00:09:25.767
♪ (crowd) Hide it! ♪

153
00:09:25.767 --> 00:09:27.967
♪ Stateless or stateful, to a client? ♪

154
00:09:27.967 --> 00:09:28.967
♪ (crowd) Show it! ♪

155
00:09:28.967 --> 00:09:33.327
♪ Split your code by steps, or by decisions? ♪

156
00:09:33.327 --> 00:09:34.367
♪ (crowd) Decisions! ♪

157
00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:40.529
(narrator) The final chorus: Parnas’s sentence again, and the three modules with their secrets.

158
00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:48.611
(narrator) The split module is now UserRepository: its interface, find by email, stays the same while its secret changes from MongoDB to Supabase.

159
00:09:48.611 --> 00:09:51.245
(narrator) Only one module changes, and confetti flies.

160
00:09:51.833 --> 00:09:54.703
♪ We propose that one begins ♪

161
00:09:54.703 --> 00:09:57.473
♪ with a list of difficult design decisions ♪

162
00:09:57.473 --> 00:10:00.313
♪ or design decisions which are likely to change. ♪

163
00:10:00.313 --> 00:10:02.473
♪ Each module has been designed ♪

164
00:10:02.473 --> 00:10:06.173
